WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.160
Let's say I am the lead

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.600
data engineer. I built this ontology,

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.620
so I have administrative access to

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.939
everything. I ask it about revenue

00:00:07.939 --> 00:00:09.380
and inventory across the entire global

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.180
company, and it gives me a

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.280
comprehensive answer. Now, I share

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.800
that exact same Fabric ontology file

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.020
with a regional manager who only

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:18.820
handles the West Coast.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.120
If the West Coast Regional Manager

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.900
logs in and asks the exact

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.300
same verbatim question, show me all

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.040
global revenue and inventory risk,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.259
the agent will only return data

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:30.320
for the West Coast.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.939
When it really, even with the

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:32.900
same prompt. Yes.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.700
Because when the AI attempts to

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.360
execute the SQL query against the

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.320
underlying Lakehouse, the database

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.580
recognizes the manager's identity and

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.019
enforces row -level security, blocking

00:00:43.019 --> 00:00:44.559
access to the East Coast data.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:45.919
That is so smart.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.420
The source documentation is explicit

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.220
on this point.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:50.860
Sharing an ontology with a user

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.820
does not magically bypass workspace

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.839
item permissions or database security.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.660
So an engineer successfully querying

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.880
a source during testing does not

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.300
guarantee that an end user will

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.120
see the same results.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.960
Exactly. You have to validate the

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.280
entire workflow using actual end user

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.600
test identities, not just the developer's

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.480
God Mode account.
